%N A402508 Number of integer lattice points after n rounds, starting with (0,0) and (2,0), where each round adds all integer points on every circle whose diameter joins two distinct points present at the start of that round.
%C A402508 Let S_n be the set of points after n rounds, so S_0 = {(0,0), (2,0)} and a(n) is the number of points of S_n. S_(n+1) consists of S_n and all integer points of the circles whose diameters join two distinct points A, B of S_n. A point X lies on the circle with diameter AB exactly when the dot product (X-A).(X-B) is 0; for X other than A and B this says that the angle AXB is a right angle (Thales's theorem and its converse).
%C A402508 Let O = (1,0), the midpoint of the two starting points. For n >= 1, S_n has the eight symmetries of a square centered at O with sides parallel to the axes, and its farthest points are at distance exactly 2^((n-1)/2) from O (proved in the notes file). For k >= 1, S_(2k) contains all integer points (x,y) of the square max(|x-1|, |y|) <= 2^(k-1), and S_(2k+1) contains those of the diamond |x-1| + |y| <= 2^k (proved in the notes file); this gives the lower bounds in the Formula section. S_n is exactly this square or diamond for 2 <= n <= 6 and strictly larger for 7 <= n <= 27 (both computed; see the Example section for n = 5 and n = 7).
%C A402508 For n >= 64, every integer point within distance (1 - 16/n)*2^((n-1)/2) of O is in S_n (proved in the notes file). Together with the distance 2^((n-1)/2) of the farthest points from O and Gauss's elementary estimate for the number of integer points in a disk, this gives the two-sided bound for n >= 64 and the limit in the Formula section, and it shows that the share of the integer points of the closed disk of radius 2^((n-1)/2) around O that lie in S_n tends to 1. This share increases from about 80% at n = 6 to about 97.5% at n = 27 (computed), and a(n)/2^n increases for 7 <= n <= 27 (computed); it is not known whether a(n)/2^n increases for all n >= 7.
%C A402508 Starting instead from (0,0) and (m,0), m >= 1, no point is ever added if m is in A004614 (all prime factors congruent to 3 mod 4), and the set after n rounds is (m/2)*S_n, with a(n) points, if m/2 is in A004614, that is, for m = 2, 6, 14, 18, 22, ...; for every m not in A004614 the points of all rounds together form the lattice of points with both coordinates divisible by A097706(m) (proved in the notes file, with Fermat's two-squares theorem).
%C A402508 Circles on diameters between given points are drawn once, not iterated, in A358746 and A360350; A333944 iterates straightedge-and-compass constructions. See the notes file in the Links section for proofs, sources and further data.
%H A402508 Alper Ferudun, <a href="/A402508/a402508.txt">Notes, proofs and sources</a>.
%H A402508 Alper Ferudun, <a href="/A402508/a402508_1.txt">Rust program</a>.
%H A402508 Alper Ferudun, <a href="/A402508/a402508.png">The points after 12 rounds and the other integer points of the disk of radius 2^(11/2) around (1,0)</a>.
%F A402508 For k >= 1, a(2k) >= A016754(2^(k-1)) = (2^k+1)^2 and a(2k+1) >= A001844(2^k) = 2^(2k+1) + 2^(k+1) + 1.
%F A402508 a(n) <= A057655(2^(n-1)) for n >= 1, with equality for n = 2, 3, 4 and for no other n <= 27.
%F A402508 a(n) == 1 (mod 4) for n >= 2, since (1,0) is added in round 2 and the other points of S_n fall into orbits of four under the quarter turns about (1,0).
%F A402508 (1 - 16/n - 2^(-n/2))^2 <= a(n)/(Pi*2^(n-1)) <= (1 + 2^(-n/2))^2 for n >= 64.
%F A402508 lim_{n->oo} a(n)/2^n = Pi/2 (A019669).
%e A402508 At n = 0 there are the two points (0,0), (2,0). Round 1 uses the circle (x-1)^2 + y^2 = 1 and adds (1,1), (1,-1), giving a(1) = 4.
%e A402508 Round 2: the pairs {(2,0),(1,1)}, {(0,0),(1,1)}, {(2,0),(1,-1)}, {(0,0),(1,-1)} each give (1,0) and, respectively, (2,1), (0,1), (2,-1), (0,-1); the pairs {(0,0),(2,0)} and {(1,1),(1,-1)} give the circle of round 1 again. The result is the 3 X 3 square 0 <= x <= 2, -1 <= y <= 1, so a(2) = 9.
%e A402508 Round 3 adds (-1,0) from {(0,1),(0,-1)}, (3,0) from {(2,1),(2,-1)}, (1,2) from {(0,1),(2,1)} and (1,-2) from {(0,-1),(2,-1)}. No other point can appear, since all points of S_3 lie within distance 2 of (1,0) and that disk contains only 13 integer points, the 9 of S_2 and the 4 just added; so a(3) = 13.
%e A402508 In round 5, X = (4,2), at distance sqrt(13) < 4 from (1,0), is not added: S_4 is the square -1 <= x <= 3, -2 <= y <= 2, so for all A, B in S_4 the vectors X-A and X-B have positive x-coordinate and nonnegative y-coordinate, and (X-A).(X-B) > 0. By symmetry its 8 images are not added either; they are the only integer points of the disk of radius 4 around (1,0) outside the diamond |x-1| + |y| <= 4, so S_5 is that diamond and a(5) = 41.
%e A402508 In round 7, X = (4,6) is added: A = (5,4) and B = (0,4) are in S_6, the square -3 <= x <= 5, -4 <= y <= 4, and (X-A).(X-B) = (-1,2).(4,2) = 0; likewise (3,7) is added, from (5,4) and (-3,3). The images of (4,6) and (3,7) under the 8 symmetries about (1,0) are the 16 points of S_7 outside the diamond |x-1| + |y| <= 8, which has 145 points, so a(7) = 161.
%o A402508 (Python)
%o A402508 # Prints a(0), ..., a(12).
%o A402508 from math import isqrt
%o A402508 S = {(0, 0), (2, 0)}
%o A402508 print(len(S), flush=True)
%o A402508 for t in range(1, 13):
%o A402508     D = 4 * max((x-1)**2 + y*y for x, y in S)
%o A402508     L = isqrt(D)
%o A402508     circles = [[] for _ in range(D+1)]
%o A402508     for u in range(-L, L+1):
%o A402508         H = isqrt(D-u*u)
%o A402508         for v in range(-H, H+1):
%o A402508             circles[u*u+v*v].append((u, v))
%o A402508     P = sorted(S)
%o A402508     U = set(S)
%o A402508     for i, (a, b) in enumerate(P):
%o A402508         for c, d in P[i+1:]:
%o A402508             for u, v in circles[(a-c)**2+(b-d)**2]:
%o A402508                 if (a+c+u) % 2 == 0 and (b+d+v) % 2 == 0:
%o A402508                     U.add(((a+c+u)//2, (b+d+v)//2))
%o A402508     S = U
%o A402508     print(len(S), flush=True)
